{"name":"Esoteric","hex":"#c4b5a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"esoteric","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Intended for or likely to be understood by only a small number of people with a specialized knowledge or interest, or an enlightened inner circle.","Having to do with concepts that are highly theoretical and without obvious practical application; often with mystical or religious connotations."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An esoteric doctrine or treatise; esoteric philosophy.","One who believes, or is an initiate, in esoteric doctrines or rites."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/esoteric"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eastern esotericism","blocks":[{"text":"Eastern esotericism is a term used by some scholars that loosely encompasses religious beliefs and practices of the Eastern world said to be \"esoteric\", secret, or occult. Its demarcation as a field, however, is difficult, as it varies depending on the boundaries of geographical and cultural notions of Western and Eastern and the definition of esotericism, with some scholars arguing it cannot be a concept beyond Western esotericism, while others propose a globalizing perspective. Still, it has been employed by scholars who recognize the category, used to denote comparable secret studies and practices, mainly in traditions of Hinduism and Buddhism, and also in other systems, ethnic religions, and syncretisms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Adi-Buddha_Samantabhadra.jpg/330px-Adi-Buddha_Samantabhadra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_esotericism"},{"title":"Esoteric (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Esoteric is an English funeral doom metal band from Birmingham, founded in 1992. Releasing seven studio albums, and a demo, the band is widely regarded as among the first groups to develop the funeral doom style. The band has named a variety of groups as influences, such as metal bands Morbid Angel and My Dying Bride, more \"trippy\" artists such as Pink Floyd, Spacemen 3, and King Crimson, and the industrial and dark ambient genres."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Esoteric_-_Brutal_Assault_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Esoteric_-_Brutal_Assault_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esoteric_(band)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Enki%28Ea%29.jpg/250px-Enki%28Ea%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Enki%28Ea%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Enki%28Ea%29.jpg/250px-Enki%28Ea%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Enki, guardian of Me, god of groundwater and wisdom, with fish flowing from his shoulders and an eagle descending in front of him. Seal of Adda (ca. 2300 BCE, Akkadian Empire)[40][41]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEnki(Ea).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Mesopotamian_cylinder_seal_impression.jpg/500px-Mesopotamian_cylinder_seal_impression.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Mesopotamian_cylinder_seal_impression.jpg/500px-Mesopotamian_cylinder_seal_impression.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mesopotamian seal from the first millennium B.C., showing a sage dressed as a fish[44] and a worshiper in front of him, as well as celestial representations such as the winged solar disk symbol, a crescent moon, and a cluster of stars","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMesopotamian_cylinder_seal_impression.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Symbolic_scene_-_king_Ashurnasirpal.jpg/500px-Symbolic_scene_-_king_Ashurnasirpal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Symbolic_scene_-_king_Ashurnasirpal.jpg/1280px-Symbolic_scene_-_king_Ashurnasirpal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Symbolic_scene_-_king_Ashurnasirpal.jpg/500px-Symbolic_scene_-_king_Ashurnasirpal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Symbolic scene of Ashurbanipal in ritual clothing, in front of a sacred tree, accompanied by winged jinn (865-860 BCE)[52]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASymbolic_scene_-_king_Ashurnasirpal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Sogdian-Zoroastrian_Deities%2C_Tunhwang.jpg/500px-Sogdian-Zoroastrian_Deities%2C_Tunhwang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Sogdian-Zoroastrian_Deities%2C_Tunhwang.jpg/1280px-Sogdian-Zoroastrian_Deities%2C_Tunhwang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Sogdian-Zoroastrian_Deities%2C_Tunhwang.jpg/500px-Sogdian-Zoroastrian_Deities%2C_Tunhwang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sogdian Daenas (10th century), associated with the Zoroastrian cult in Sogdia. The figure on the left is considered to be Daena, while the one on the right is thought to be Daeva[57]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASogdian-Zoroastrian_Deities%2C_Tunhwang.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Jesus_as_a_Manichaean_Prophet%2